{"step_index":15,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:43:41Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-23T22:13:41+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-23T22:13:41+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/Downloads/find-skill-repo/all%20github%20repos/mempalace/README.md`\nTotal Lines: 280\nTotal Bytes: 11960\nShowing lines 1 to 280\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: <div align=\"center\">\r\n2: \r\n3: <img src=\"assets/mempalace_logo.png\" alt=\"MemPalace\" width=\"240\">\r\n4: \r\n5: # MemPalace\r\n6: \r\n7: Local-first AI memory. Verbatim storage, pluggable backend, 96.6% R@5 raw on LongMemEval — zero API calls.\r\n8: \r\n9: [![][version-shield]][release-link]\r\n10: [![][python-shield]][python-link]\r\n11: [![][license-shield]][license-link]\r\n12: [![][discord-shield]][discord-link]\r\n13: \r\n14: </div>\r\n15: \r\n16: > [!CAUTION]\r\n17: > **Beware of impostor sites.** MemPalace has no other official websites. The **only** official sources are this **[GitHub repository](https://github.com/MemPalace/mempalace)**, the **[PyPI package](https://pypi.org/project/mempalace/)**, and the docs at **[mempalaceofficial.com](https://mempalaceofficial.com)**. Any other domain (including `.tech`, `.net`, or other `.com` variants) is an impostor and may distribute malware. Details and timeline: [docs/HISTORY.md](docs/HISTORY.md).\r\n18: \r\n19: > [!IMPORTANT]\r\n20: > **Claude Code sessions expire in 30 days without auto-save hooks wired.** [Read this →](https://github.com/MemPalace/mempalace/discussions/1388)\r\n21: >\r\n22: > Need the shortest recovery/setup path? Use the [Claude Code retention setup checklist](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/claude-code-retention.html).\r\n23: \r\n24: ---\r\n25: \r\n26: ## What it is\r\n27: \r\n28: MemPalace stores your conversation history as verbatim text and retrieves\r\n29: it with semantic search. It does not summarize, extract, or paraphrase.\r\n30: The index is structured — people and projects become *wings*, topics\r\n31: become *rooms*, and original content lives in *drawers* — so searches\r\n32: can be scoped rather than run against a flat corpus.\r\n33: \r\n34: The retrieval layer is pluggable. The current default is ChromaDB; the\r\n35: interface is defined in [`mempalace/backends/base.py`](mempalace/backends/base.py)\r\n36: and alternative backends can be dropped in without touching the rest of\r\n37: the system.\r\n38: \r\n39: Nothing leaves your machine unless you opt in.\r\n40: \r\n41: Architecture, concepts, and mining flows:\r\n42: [mempalaceofficial.com/concepts/the-palace](https://mempalaceofficial.com/concepts/the-palace.html).\r\n43: \r\n44: ---\r\n45: \r\n46: ## Install\r\n47: \r\n48: MemPalace ships a CLI, so install it in an isolated environment to avoid\r\n49: PEP 668 errors on Debian/Ubuntu/Homebrew Pythons and to keep mempalace's\r\n50: deps (`chromadb`, `numpy`, `grpcio`, …) from conflicting with anything\r\n51: else in your global site-packages.\r\n52: \r\n53: We recommend [`uv`](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/) — `uv tool install` puts\r\n54: the `mempalace` CLI in an isolated environment on your PATH:\r\n55: \r\n56: ```bash\r\n57: uv tool install mempalace\r\n58: mempalace init ~/projects/myapp\r\n59: ```\r\n60: \r\n61: [`pipx`](https://pipx.pypa.io/) works the same way if you prefer it:\r\n62: `pipx install mempalace`.\r\n63: \r\n64: Prefer plain `pip` only inside an activated virtualenv where you\r\n65: explicitly want `import mempalace` available:\r\n66: \r\n67: ```bash\r\n68: python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\r\n69: pip install mempalace\r\n70: ```\r\n71: \r\n72: ### Docker\r\n73: \r\n74: A container image is also available for running the MCP server or the CLI\r\n75: without a local Python toolchain. Everything persists under `/data` (palace,\r\n76: config, and the cached embedding model), so mount a volume there.\r\n77: \r\n78: ```bash\r\n79: # Build the image (CPU; bundles the `extract` + `spellcheck` extras)\r\n80: docker build -t mempalace .\r\n81: \r\n82: # MCP server over stdio — note the `-i` flag (JSON-RPC needs stdin)\r\n83: docker run -i --rm -v mempalace-data:/data mempalace\r\n84: \r\n85: # Run any CLI command instead (mount the host directory you want to mine)\r\n86: docker run --rm -v mempalace-data:/data -v /path/to/project:/work mempalace mine /work\r\n87: docker run --rm -v mempalace-data:/data mempalace search \"why GraphQL\"\r\n88: ```\r\n89: \r\n90: Wire it into an MCP client (e.g. Claude Code) as a stdio server:\r\n91: \r\n92: ```json\r\n93: {\r\n94:   \"mcpServers\": {\r\n95:     \"mempalace\": {\r\n96:       \"command\": \"docker\",\r\n97:       \"args\": [\"run\", \"-i\", \"--rm\", \"-v\", \"mempalace-data:/data\", \"mempalace\"]\r\n98:     }\r\n99:   }\r\n100: }\r\n101: ```\r\n102: \r\n103: `docker compose run --rm mcp` works too (see `docker-compose.yml`). For\r\n104: CUDA-accelerated embeddings, build the GPU variant with\r\n105: `docker build -f Dockerfile.gpu -t mempalace:gpu .` and run it with\r\n106: `--gpus all`. Customise the bundled extras at build time, e.g.\r\n107: `docker build --build-arg EXTRAS=\"extract,spellcheck\" -t mempalace .`.\r\n108: \r\n109: ## Storage backends\r\n110: \r\n111: ChromaDB is the default and needs no configuration. MemPalace also ships a\r\n112: pluggable backend contract, exercised across deliberately different substrates\r\n113: so the contract is never accidentally shaped around one vendor. Every\r\n114: non-default backend is opt-in.\r\n115: \r\n116: | Backend | Mode | Install | Namespaces | Lexical | Configure with |\r\n117: | ------- | ---- | ------- | :--------: | :-----: | -------------- |\r\n118: | `chroma` _(default)_ | Local (embedded) | bundled | – | ✓ | – |\r\n119: | `sqlite_exact` | Local (exact) | bundled | – | ✓ | – |\r\n120: | `milvus` | Local (Lite) · Server opt-in | `mempalace[milvus]` | ✓ | ✓ | `MEMPALACE_MILVUS_URI` |\r\n121: | `qdrant` | Server (REST) | bundled | ✓ | ✓ | `MEMPALACE_QDRANT_URL` |\r\n122: | `pgvector` | Server (Postgres) | `mempalace[pgvector]` | ✓ | ✓ | `MEMPALACE_PGVECTOR_DSN` |\r\n123: \r\n124: Select with `--backend <name>`, `MEMPALACE_BACKEND=<name>`, or\r\n125: `\"backend\": \"<name>\"` in `config.json`. See\r\n126: [Storage backends](/guide/configuration#storage-backends) for connection\r\n127: variables, namespace behavior, and deployment notes.\r\n128: \r\n129: ## Quickstart\r\n130: \r\n131: ```bash\r\n132: # Mine content into the palace\r\n133: mempalace mine ~/projects/myapp                    # project files\r\n134: mempalace mine ~/.claude/projects/ --mode convos   # Claude Code sessions (scope with --wing per project)\r\n135: \r\n136: # Search\r\n137: mempalace search \"why did we switch to GraphQL\"\r\n138: \r\n139: # Load context for a new session\r\n140: mempalace wake-up\r\n141: ```\r\n142: \r\n143: For Claude Code, Gemini CLI, [Antigravity](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/antigravity.html),\r\n144: MCP-compatible tools, and local models, see\r\n145: [mempalaceofficial.com/guide/getting-started](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/getting-started.html).\r\n146: \r\n147: ---\r\n148: \r\n149: ## Benchmarks\r\n150: \r\n151: All numbers below are reproducible from this repository with the commands\r\n152: in [`benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md`](benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md). Full\r\n153: per-question result files are committed under `benchmarks/results_*`.\r\n154: \r\n155: **LongMemEval — retrieval recall (R@5, 500 questions):**\r\n156: \r\n157: | Mode | R@5 | LLM required |\r\n158: |---|---|---|\r\n159: | Raw (semantic search, no heuristics, no LLM) | **96.6%** | None |\r\n160: | Hybrid v4, held-out 450q (tuned on 50 dev, not seen during training) | **98.4%** | None |\r\n161: | Hybrid v4 + LLM rerank (full 500) | ≥99% | Any capable model |\r\n162: \r\n163: The raw 96.6% requires no API key, no cloud, and no LLM at any stage. The\r\n164: hybrid pipeline adds keyword boosting, temporal-proximity boosting, and\r\n165: preference-pattern extraction; the held-out 98.4% is the honest\r\n166: generalisable figure.\r\n167: \r\n168: The rerank pipeline promotes the best candidate out of the top-20\r\n169: retrieved sessions using an LLM reader. It works with any reasonably\r\n170: capable model — we have reproduced it with Claude Haiku, Claude Sonnet,\r\n171: and minimax-m2.7 via Ollama Cloud (no Anthropic dependency). The gap\r\n172: between raw and reranked is model-agnostic; we do not headline a \"100%\"\r\n173: number because the last 0.6% was reached by inspecting specific wrong\r\n174: answers, which `benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md` flags as teaching to the test.\r\n175: \r\n176: **Other benchmarks (full results in [`benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md`](benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md)):**\r\n177: \r\n178: | Benchmark | Metric | Score | Notes |\r\n179: |---|---|---|---|\r\n180: | LoCoMo (session, top-10, no rerank) | R@10 | 60.3% | 1,986 questions |\r\n181: | LoCoMo (hybrid v5, top-10, no rerank) | R@10 | 88.9% | Same set |\r\n182: | ConvoMem (all categories, 250 items) | Avg recall | 92.9% | 50 per category |\r\n183: | MemBench (ACL 2025, 8,500 items) | R@5 | 80.3% | All categories |\r\n184: \r\n185: We deliberately do not include a side-by-side comparison against Mem0,\r\n186: Mastra, Hindsight, Supermemory, or Zep. Those projects publish different\r\n187: metrics on different splits, and placing retrieval recall next to\r\n188: end-to-end QA accuracy is not an honest comparison. See each project's\r\n189: own research page for their published numbers.\r\n190: \r\n191: **Reproducing every result:**\r\n192: \r\n193: ```bash\r\n194: git clone https://github.com/MemPalace/mempalace.git\r\n195: cd mempalace\r\n196: uv sync --extra dev   # or: pip install -e \".[dev]\"\r\n197: # see benchmarks/README.md for dataset download commands\r\n198: uv run python benchmarks/longmemeval_bench.py /path/to/longmemeval_s_cleaned.json\r\n199: ```\r\n200: \r\n201: ---\r\n202: \r\n203: ## Knowledge graph\r\n204: \r\n205: MemPalace includes a temporal entity-relationship graph with validity\r\n206: windows — add, query, invalidate, timeline — backed by local SQLite.\r\n207: Usage and tool reference:\r\n208: [mempalaceofficial.com/concepts/knowledge-graph](https://mempalaceofficial.com/concepts/knowledge-graph.html).\r\n209: \r\n210: ## MCP server\r\n211: \r\n212: 36 MCP tools cover palace reads/writes, knowledge-graph operations,\r\n213: cross-wing navigation, drawer management, and agent diaries. Installation\r\n214: and the full tool list:\r\n215: [mempalaceofficial.com/reference/mcp-tools](https://mempalaceofficial.com/reference/mcp-tools.html).\r\n216: \r\n217: ## Agents\r\n218: \r\n219: Each specialist agent gets its own wing and diary in the palace.\r\n220: Discoverable at runtime via `mempalace_list_agents` — no bloat in your\r\n221: system prompt:\r\n222: [mempalaceofficial.com/concepts/agents](https://mempalaceofficial.com/concepts/agents.html).\r\n223: \r\n224: ## Auto-save hooks\r\n225: \r\n226: Auto-save hooks for **Claude Code, Codex CLI, and Cursor IDE** save\r\n227: periodically and before context compression:\r\n228: \r\n229: - Claude Code + Codex →\r\n230:   [mempalaceofficial.com/guide/hooks](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/hooks.html)\r\n231: - Cursor IDE (adds session-start recall and a transcript snapshot before\r\n232:   compaction) →\r\n233:   [mempalaceofficial.com/guide/cursor-hooks](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/cursor-hooks.html)\r\n234: \r\n235: If you are installing under time pressure, start with the\r\n236: [Claude Code retention setup checklist](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/claude-code-retention.html):\r\n237: wire the hooks, back up existing JSONL transcripts, and backfill them with\r\n238: `mempalace mine ~/.claude/projects/ --mode convos`.\r\n239: \r\n240: For per-message recall on top of the file-level chunks the hooks produce,\r\n241: run `mempalace sweep <transcript-dir>` periodically — it stores one\r\n242: verbatim drawer per user/assistant message, idempotent and resume-safe.\r\n243: \r\n244: ---\r\n245: \r\n246: ## Requirements\r\n247: \r\n248: - Python 3.9+\r\n249: - A vector-store backend (ChromaDB by default)\r\n250: - ~300 MB disk for the embedding model. Onboarding (`python -m mempalace.onboarding`) offers `embeddinggemma-300m` (multilingual, 100+ languages, recommended) or `all-MiniLM-L6-v2` (English-only, ~30 MB). See the docstring at [`mempalace/embedding.py`](mempalace/embedding.py) for details and migration notes.\r\n251: \r\n252: No API key is required for the core benchmark path.\r\n253: \r\n254: ## Docs\r\n255: \r\n256: - Getting started → [mempalaceofficial.com/guide/getting-started](https://mempalaceofficial.com/guide/getting-started.html)\r\n257: - CLI reference → [mempalaceofficial.com/reference/cli](https://mempalaceofficial.com/reference/cli.html)\r\n258: - Python API → [mempalaceofficial.com/reference/python-api](https://mempalaceofficial.com/reference/python-api.html)\r\n259: - Full benchmark methodology → [benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md](benchmarks/BENCHMARKS.md)\r\n260: - Release notes → [CHANGELOG.md](CHANGELOG.md)\r\n261: - Corrections and public notices → [docs/HISTORY.md](docs/HISTORY.md)\r\n262: \r\n263: ## Contributing\r\n264: \r\n265: PRs welcome. See [CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md).\r\n266: \r\n267: ## License\r\n268: \r\n269: MIT — see [LICENSE](LICENSE).\r\n270: \r\n271: <!-- Link Definitions -->\r\n272: [version-shield]: https://img.shields.io/badge/version-3.7.0-4dc9f6?style=flat-square&labelColor=0a0e14\r\n273: [release-link]: https://github.com/MemPalace/mempalace/releases\r\n274: [python-shield]: https://img.shields.io/badge/python-3.9+-7dd8f8?style=flat-square&labelColor=0a0e14&logo=python&logoColor=7dd8f8\r\n275: [python-link]: https://www.python.org/\r\n276: [license-shield]: https://img.shields.io/badge/license-MIT-b0e8ff?style=flat-square&labelColor=0a0e14\r\n277: [license-link]: https://github.com/MemPalace/mempalace/blob/main/LICENSE\r\n278: [discord-shield]: https://img.shields.io/badge/discord-join-5865F2?style=flat-square&labelColor=0a0e14&logo=discord&logoColor=5865F2\r\n279: [discord-link]: https://discord.com/invite/ycTQQCu6kn\r\n280: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":16,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-23T16:43:41Z","thinking":"**Analyzing MemPalace's Design**\n\nI've been digging into MemPalace, specifically its design. I see that it prioritizes local storage and user privacy, which is appealing. I am also assessing its structured semantic retrieval system, looking at the role of lightweight embeddings and the storage of verbatim conversation histories and project knowledge.\n\n\n**Assessing MemPalace's Resources**\n\nI've been examining MemPalace's resource demands. Notably, it doesn't require a dedicated GPU or extensive RAM for local operation; a standard CPU and minimal RAM suffice, even with lightweight embedding models. GPU acceleration is an optional addition in Docker builds. I've also begun to review the system's resource requirements for the six projects, hyperframes, airi, MoneyPrinterTurbo, OpenMontage, OpenReels, and mempalace, to ascertain local hardware needs.\n\n\n**Evaluating Project Hardware Needs**\n\nI'm now diving into the specific resource needs of the individual projects integrated into MemPalace. I've noted that AIRI, MoneyPrinterTurbo, and OpenMontage all operate locally with minimal hardware requirements, largely relying on cloud APIs for intensive tasks. AIRI works in-browser or Electron. MoneyPrinterTurbo works with 4GB RAM on the CPU. OpenMontage uses Remotion and FFmpeg, requiring a dedicated GPU only for specific local models. All of them use Node.js.\n\n\n**Reviewing OpenReels and MemPalace**\n\nI've just reviewed OpenReels and MemPalace in terms of their hardware requirements. OpenReels, a TypeScript video pipeline, is lightweight, relying on Node.js, Remotion, and Docker for CPU-based operations, while cloud APIs handle the heavy lifting. MemPalace itself is also resource-efficient, requiring only a CPU and minimal RAM via compact embedding models and ChromaDB/SQLite. Both appear well-suited for local operation.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"send_message","args":{"Message":"Here is the summary and hardware requirement assessment for each of the 6 repositories:\n\n### 1. **HyperFrames**\n* **Summary:** HyperFrames is an open-source video rendering framework that turns plain HTML, CSS, media, and seekable web animations (GSAP, Lottie, Three.js) into deterministic MP4 videos using headless Chrome (Puppeteer) and FFmpeg.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** It runs lightweight on CPU via Node.js (≥22) and FFmpeg without needing a dedicated GPU or high RAM.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Project AIRI**\n* **Summary:** Project AIRI is an open-source AI VTuber and digital companion platform (inspired by Neuro-sama) integrating LLMs, Live2D/VRM avatars, speech recognition/synthesis, and autonomous gameplay capabilities across web, desktop, and mobile.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** It operates directly in web browsers or Electron via WebGPU/WASM and offloads heavy AI inference (LLM, TTS, ASR) to cloud APIs by default, though optional local GPU acceleration (via CUDA/Metal/Ollama) is supported if desired.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **MoneyPrinterTurbo**\n* **Summary:** MoneyPrinterTurbo is an all-in-one AI short-video creation tool that takes a topic or keyword to automatically generate scripts, source matching stock/local video footage, synthesize speech and subtitles, and produce HD short-form videos.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** The default setup requires only 4 GB RAM and a standard CPU (using cloud LLMs and Edge TTS); a dedicated GPU (4GB–8GB+ VRAM) is purely optional for local `faster-whisper` transcription or intensive batch rendering.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **OpenMontage**\n* **Summary:** OpenMontage is an open-source, agentic video production system that enables AI coding assistants to conduct live research, write scripts, generate assets, and assemble finished videos using Remotion, HyperFrames, and FFmpeg.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** The standard pipeline runs smoothly on CPU using Node.js, FFmpeg, Piper TTS, and cloud generation APIs or stock media; a dedicated GPU is only needed if choosing to run local video models (such as Wan 2.1 or Hunyuan).\n\n---\n\n### 5. **OpenReels**\n* **Summary:** OpenReels is a TypeScript-based AI production pipeline and Web UI that turns any prompt or topic into a publish-ready vertical short video with AI-generated research, script, voiceover, visuals, soundtrack, and Remotion rendering in a single command.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** All heavy generation tasks (LLM, images, video, music, TTS) are routed through cloud APIs (Google Gemini, OpenAI, Anthropic, ElevenLabs, fal.ai), while the local app runs on standard CPU memory via Node.js/Docker and Remotion.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **MemPalace**\n* **Summary:** MemPalace is a local-first, zero-cloud AI memory system that stores conversation histories and project knowledge verbatim in structured \"wings\" and \"drawers\" for high-accuracy semantic retrieval without summarization.\n* **Requires Dedicated GPU / Heavy RAM locally?** **No.** It runs completely on CPU and minimal memory using ChromaDB/SQLite and lightweight local embedding models (~30 MB to ~300 MB disk space).","Recipient":"b75d3ef6-c7ea-497e-a92f-fe05edd5e5c3","toolAction":"Sending research report to caller","toolSummary":"Report repository findings"}}]}
